#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# replace_list

#% text
# `replace_list(e, rules)` gives the list of the results of every way a rule matches the whole of `e`; `replace_list(e, rules, n)` at most `n` of them.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The rules are tried in order and each rule's matches in the matcher's enumeration order (sequence variables shortest first).

#% item
# `replace_list(rules)(e)` is the operator form.

#% item
# Under an associative-commutative head a single pattern takes one argument, so `x_ + y_` does not match `a + b + c`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
replace_list([a, b, c], [x__, y__] -> [x, y])
#% output
#= [[[a], [b, c]], [[a, b], [c]]]

#% cell
replace_list([a, b, b, b, c, c, a], [___, x_, x_, ___] -> x)
#% output
#= [b, b, c]

#% cell
replace_list(x, [x -> a, x -> b, x -> c])
#% output
#= [a, b, c]

#% section
# See also

#% text
